rade_c — RADE, FreeDV's radio autoencoder, in C: versions 1 and 2.

    https://github.com/freedv/rade_c        BSD 2-clause licence, see LICENSE

RADE is by David Rowe, VK5DGR; the C port is the FreeDV team's, from the
Python of https://github.com/drowe67/radae. Speech, as the features a
vocoder works in, is put by a neural encoder onto an OFDM signal that fits
an SSB passband, and a neural decoder takes it off again.

These are the files of the library, rade_c's src/ as its CMakeLists builds
the "rade" library of them, at its commit c8a3dc1 of 7 October 2026. Its
tools, tests and CMake files are not carried. One change is made here, and
is to be offered to FreeDV:

    rade_rx_v2.c, rade_rx_v2.h   the receiver follows a drift in timing

Once in sync, version 2's receiver followed the timing with a one-term
IIR of 1000 symbols, 20 s, which falls behind a steady drift by the drift
times 1000 symbols. Two radios' sound cards differ by their crystals; on
14 October 2026 an sbitx and a zbitx were 229 ppm apart, the timing fell
37 samples behind, past the cyclic prefix of 32, and the speech broke up
some 20 s into each over and came back with the next. A second term now
follows the rate of the drift, so that a steady drift leaves no lag. The
change is marked "zbitxre" in rade_rx_v2.c, which says more. Moving to a
newer rade_c, see whether FreeDV has done this, and if not, carry it.

The radio offers both versions: version 1, which FreeDV has released, as
the mode RADE1, and version 2 as RADE2; modems/modem_rade.c says why.

It is built on Opus's neural network code, which is in modems/opus_dnn.

FOUR OF THE FILES ARE NOT IN THE REPOSITORY

The weights of the networks, 112 MB of numbers as C:

    rade_enc_data.c     rade_dec_data.c         version 1
    rade_enc_v2_data.c  rade_dec_v2_data.c      version 2

FreeDV says version 2's waveform and weights may still change, and that a
later version will not work with this one. So they are not kept in git, to
be carried in its history for ever; setup/fetch_rade_weights.sh fetches
them from the commit named above and checks each against its SHA-256. The
build says so if they are not there.

For the same reason this is not to go on the air for operators until
FreeDV releases version 2: rade_c's README asks that it not.

To move to a newer rade_c: copy the same files from it, change the commit
here and the commit and the sums in setup/fetch_rade_weights.sh, fetch,
and run make check, which has FreeDV's own measure of whether the codec
is whole, tests/test_rade_codec.c.
